---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import WorkDiagram from '../../components/WorkDiagram.astro';
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro';

const stages = [
  {
    skill: 'lets-work',
    title: 'opens the work',
    does: 'Always the first step. You pick a short id and say what already exists: an RFC in Notion, specs, Jira tickets, an issue. Each is saved verbatim and mapped in, ready to be challenged.',
    writes: ['work.md'],
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-research',
    title: 'finds out what is true first',
    does: 'A spike: Slack threads, Notion pages, tickets and code, each saved with verbatim excerpts, then the findings tagged by origin, with contradictions and unknowns, and no recommendation.',
    writes: ['sources.md', 'research.md'],
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-rfc',
    title: 'interviews you, then writes the RFC',
    does: 'Rounds of questions from the research. Each point becomes your verdict, a product gap for the tickets, or a question for reviewers, and your answer is on disk before the next round. Reviewer comments come back as numbered rounds.',
    writes: ['rfc.md', 'rfc.log.md', 'rfc-reviews/R1/round.md'],
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-specs',
    title: 'one small spec at a time',
    does: 'It proposes the list, you reshape it, then it challenges each spec with you and writes only what you decided.',
    writes: ['specs/INDEX.md', 'specs/S-01-….md', 'specs/S-01-….log.md'],
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-tickets',
    title: 'cuts tickets you can check',
    does: 'Every acceptance criterion names how it is verified: a command, a test, or an observable behaviour.',
    writes: ['tickets/T-001-…/ticket.md'],
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-subtasks',
    title: 'splits a ticket',
    does: 'Small tasks, each with one repo, one owning staff seat, the files it owns and a check for done.',
    writes: ['tasks.md'],
  },
  {
    title: 'You approve the task list',
    does: 'No code is written before a person has read the plan.',
    gate: true,
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-build',
    title: 'runs the tasks through the seats',
    does: 'One task at a time. An independent checker then verifies every criterion and writes the evidence into the ticket.',
    writes: ['tasks.md', 'ticket.md'],
  },
  {
    title: 'You ship a draft pull request',
    does: 'Committing and pushing stay yours, with your own commands. No skill of this bundle does either.',
    gate: true,
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-review',
    title: 'reviews your own pull request',
    does: 'Only once CI is green. Every axis, a blind reader that sees nothing but the diff, and every nit.',
    writes: ['reviews/R1/round.md'],
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-triage',
    title: 'rules on each item with you',
    does: 'Fix, reject, defer or ask, one item at a time, with your reason saved before the next one. Fixes are applied at the end.',
    writes: ['reviews/R1/round.md'],
  },
  {
    title: 'You ship the fixes, and reviewers comment',
    does: 'Each new review, yours or someone else’s, is a new numbered round handled the same way.',
    gate: true,
  },
  {
    skill: 'to-archive',
    title: 'closes the work',
    does: 'The whole directory moves to the archive. Nothing is deleted, so the reasons stay searchable.',
    writes: ['~/.work-archive/…'],
  },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Work"
  description="A file-backed workflow from RFC to merged pull request: eleven small skills that keep sources, decisions, specs, tickets and review rounds in local files, so any session resumes from disk."
  intro="Long work outlives a session. Every time a conversation is compacted or restarted, the reasons behind a decision go first. The work bundle keeps each step in local files instead: what was read, what you answered and why, what was built, what a review found and how you ruled on it."
>
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Each skill does one step, reads what it needs from disk, and writes its result back before it asks you anything else.
    None of them depends on the conversation that came before, so you can run any of them in a fresh session, in any
    order that makes sense for the work in front of you.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>What it means for you</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Write before asking</td><td>Your answers are saved before the next round of questions. A dead session loses nothing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Outcome and reason are separate files</td><td>A spec stays a two-minute read. Its log holds every question, your answer and why.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One evidence file</td><td>Every source is kept with verbatim excerpts, so a link that rots costs nothing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Nothing invented</td><td>What you did not decide is listed as an open question, never filled in.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Local only</td><td>No skill commits, pushes, posts a comment or publishes to a board. Those stay your steps.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>From an idea to a merged pull request</h2>
  <WorkDiagram
    stages={stages}
    caption="Diamonds in ochre are yours: approving the plan and shipping. Dashed tiles are the files each step writes."
  />

  <h2>Start from wherever you are</h2>
  <p>
    <code>/lets-work</code> is always the first step. It asks once what already exists, saves each item verbatim, maps it
    into the work's documents, and names the next step. The skill that owns an imported document then challenges it
    instead of writing it again. Levels you do not need are simply skipped.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Give <code>/lets-work</code></th><th>Then</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Personal project, a quick change</td><td>A description</td><td><code>/to-tickets</code>, <code>/to-subtasks</code>, <code>/to-build</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Personal project, a feature with no RFC</td><td>A description</td><td><code>/to-specs</code>, then <code>/to-tickets</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Job, a new feature from zero</td><td>The Slack threads and PRD links</td><td><code>/to-research</code>, <code>/to-rfc</code>, <code>/to-specs</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Job, the RFC is already in Notion</td><td>The Notion URL</td><td><code>/to-rfc</code> challenges it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Job, specs written before</td><td>Their paths or URLs</td><td><code>/to-specs</code> challenges them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Job, tickets already in Jira</td><td>The Jira keys</td><td><code>/to-tickets</code> challenges them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Open source, an issue you will fix</td><td>The issue URL</td><td><code>/to-tickets</code>, <code>/to-subtasks</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A session you are coming back to</td><td>Nothing: run <code>/work-status my-id</code></td><td>Whatever it names as next</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>/to-review</code> and <code>/to-triage</code> are for your own tickets. Someone else's pull request, an
    open-source one included, is reviewed with the review command instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the files live</h2>
  <p>
    Outside every repository, under one root (<code>~/.work</code> by default, or <code>WORK_ROOT</code>), keyed by the
    repository name. A git worktree maps to its real repository, so every session of a project sees the same work.
  </p>
  <Terminal
    label="One work"
    lines={[
      { kind: 'out', text: '~/.work/<repo>/<work-id>/' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  work.md' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  sources.md                 SRC-01, SRC-02, …' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  research.md   glossary.md   adr/' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  rfc.md   rfc.log.md   rfc-reviews/R1/round.md' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  specs/INDEX.md' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  specs/S-01-<slug>.md   S-01-<slug>.log.md' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  tickets/T-001-<slug>/ticket.md   ticket.log.md   tasks.md' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  tickets/T-001-<slug>/reviews/R1/round.md' },
      { kind: 'out', text: '  tickets/T-001-<slug>/reviews/R2/round.md' },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    Ids are stable and never reused: <code>SRC-04</code> for a source, <code>S-02</code> for a spec, <code>T-007</code> for
    a ticket, <code>T-007.3</code> for a task, <code>R2-F05</code> for the fifth item of a ticket's second review round.
    Every document carries its work id, so handing a skill a file is enough for it to know where it is.
  </p>

  <h2>An example, end to end</h2>
  <Terminal
    label="A feature across several sessions"
    lines={[
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/lets-work consent-v2 <slack thread> <PRD link>' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-research consent-v2 <slack thread> <notion page>' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-rfc consent-v2' },
      { kind: 'comment', text: '# reviewers commented on the RFC' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-rfc consent-v2 revise' },
      { kind: 'comment', text: '# next day, fresh session' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/work-status consent-v2' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-specs consent-v2' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-tickets consent-v2' },
      { kind: 'comment', text: '# one ticket' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-subtasks consent-v2 T-001' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-build consent-v2 T-001' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/ship' },
      { kind: 'comment', text: '# once CI is green' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-review consent-v2 T-001' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-triage consent-v2 T-001' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/ship' },
      { kind: 'comment', text: '# a teammate reviews the PR' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-triage consent-v2 T-001 comments' },
      { kind: 'comment', text: '# merged' },
      { kind: 'agent', text: '/to-archive consent-v2' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Review rounds</h2>
  <p>
    A round is one pass over a pull request, numbered in the order it happened: your own review, a second one after the
    fixes, a teammate's comments. Each lives in its own directory and is never rewritten once closed.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Ruling</th><th>Means</th><th>What is recorded</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>fix</code></td><td>Change the code</td><td>What was changed, per item</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>reject</code></td><td>Leave the code as it is</td><td>Your reason, in your words</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>defer</code></td><td>Real, but not in this pull request</td><td>The new ticket it became</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ask</code></td><td>You need an answer first</td><td>The drafted question</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Replies to reviewers are drafted in the round file. Posting them, like committing the fixes, is a step you take
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting it up</h2>
  <p>
    Add the <code>work</code> bundle to the project with kura. Because the work directory sits outside the repository,
    your agent needs permission to write there once, in your user-level settings. In Claude Code that is an
    <code>Edit(~/.work/**)</code> and an <code>Edit(~/.work-archive/**)</code> allow rule, plus the same two paths under
    <code>sandbox.filesystem.allowWrite</code>. A skill that cannot write to the root stops and says so; it never falls
    back to a directory inside the repository.
  </p>
</Docs>
